{"subjectId":"java","id":"interfaces","title":"Interfaces","group":"Inheritance & Polymorphism","order":29,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">An interface is a contract of methods a class promises to provide. A class can implement many interfaces — Java's answer to multiple inheritance.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Interface</strong> — a reference type declaring abstract methods (plus, since Java 8, <code>default</code> and <code>static</code> methods, and since Java 9, <code>private</code> methods). Fields are implicitly <code>public static final</code> constants.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-details\"></i>What an interface can contain</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Member</th><th>Implicit modifiers</th><th>Since</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Abstract method</td><td><code>public abstract</code></td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td>Constant field</td><td><code>public static final</code></td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td><code>default</code> method (has a body, inherited)</td><td><code>public</code></td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td><code>static</code> method (called on the interface only)</td><td><code>public</code></td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td><code>private</code> / <code>private static</code> helper method</td><td>—</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>Nested types</td><td><code>public static</code></td><td>1.0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Implementing several interfaces</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Duck duck = new Duck();\n        List&lt;Swimmer&gt; swimmers = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(duck, new Fish()));\n        for (Swimmer s : swimmers) s.swim();\n        Flyer f = duck;                     // a Duck is also a Flyer\n        f.fly();\n        System.out.println(Flyer.MAX_ALTITUDE);\n        System.out.println(duck instanceof Swimmer &amp;&amp; duck instanceof Flyer);\n    }\n}\n\ninterface Swimmer { void swim(); }\ninterface Flyer {\n    int MAX_ALTITUDE = 10_000;              // public static final\n    void fly();\n}\n\nclass Duck implements Swimmer, Flyer {\n    public void swim() { System.out.println(\"duck paddles\"); }   // must be public\n    public void fly()  { System.out.println(\"duck flies\"); }\n}\n\nclass Fish implements Swimmer {\n    public void swim() { System.out.println(\"fish swims\"); }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>duck paddles\nfish swims\nduck flies\n10000\ntrue</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sparkles\"></i>Default, static and private methods</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Greeter en = name -&gt; \"Hello, \" + name;    // one abstract method → lambda works\n        System.out.println(en.greet(\"Asha\"));\n        System.out.println(en.greetLoudly(\"Ravi\"));      // default method\n        System.out.println(Greeter.polite().greet(\"Meera\"));   // static factory\n    }\n}\n\ninterface Greeter {\n    String greet(String name);                  // abstract\n\n    default String greetLoudly(String name) {   // inherited, can be overridden\n        return shout(greet(name));\n    }\n\n    static Greeter polite() {                   // Greeter.polite(), not inherited by implementers\n        return name -&gt; \"Good evening, \" + name;\n    }\n\n    private String shout(String s) {            // helper shared by default methods\n        return s.toUpperCase() + \"!\";\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Hello, Asha\nHELLO, RAVI!\nGood evening, Meera</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Default methods exist so interfaces can <strong>evolve</strong>: Java 8 added <code>forEach</code>, <code>stream()</code>, <code>removeIf</code> to <code>Collection</code>/<code>Iterable</code> without breaking millions of existing implementations.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-diamond\"></i>The diamond problem with default methods</h3>\n<p>If a class inherits the same default method from two interfaces, it must override it and may pick one with <code>Interface.super.method()</code>. Rules: (1) a class method always wins over an interface default; (2) a more specific sub-interface wins; (3) otherwise you must resolve it yourself.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(new Phone().start());\n    }\n}\n\ninterface Camera { default String start() { return \"camera on\"; } }\ninterface Radio  { default String start() { return \"radio on\"; } }\n\nclass Phone implements Camera, Radio {\n    @Override public String start() {           // required: otherwise compile error\n        return Camera.super.start() + \" + \" + Radio.super.start();\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>camera on + radio on</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>interface Camera { default String start() { return \"camera on\"; } }\ninterface Radio  { default String start() { return \"radio on\"; } }\nclass Phone implements Camera, Radio { }</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>javac</figcaption><pre><code>Main.java:3: error: types Camera and Radio are incompatible;\nclass Phone implements Camera, Radio { }\n^\n  class Phone inherits unrelated defaults for start() from types Camera and Radio</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-diff\"></i>Abstract class vs interface</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Abstract class</th><th>Interface</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Instance state (fields)</td><td>Yes</td><td>No — only <code>public static final</code> constants</td></tr><tr><td>Constructors</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Multiple inheritance</td><td>No — <code>extends</code> one class</td><td>Yes — <code>implements</code> many</td></tr><tr><td>Method bodies</td><td>Any</td><td><code>default</code>, <code>static</code>, <code>private</code> only</td></tr><tr><td>Member access</td><td>Any modifier</td><td><code>public</code> (or <code>private</code> helpers)</td></tr><tr><td>Relationship</td><td>IS-A (shared identity)</td><td>CAN-DO (capability)</td></tr><tr><td>Lambda target</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, if exactly one abstract method (functional interface)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tag\"></i>Marker and functional interfaces</h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bookmark\"></i>Marker interface</h3><p>No methods at all; tags a class for special treatment: <code>Serializable</code>, <code>Cloneable</code>, <code>RandomAccess</code>. Modern code often uses annotations instead.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lambda\"></i>Functional interface</h3><p>Exactly one abstract method, so a lambda or method reference can implement it: <code>Runnable</code>, <code>Comparator</code>, <code>Function</code>. Mark with <code>@FunctionalInterface</code>.</p></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.LinkedList;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.RandomAccess;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static String strategy(List&lt;?&gt; list) {\n        return list instanceof RandomAccess ? \"index loop\" : \"iterator\";\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(strategy(new ArrayList&lt;&gt;()));\n        System.out.println(strategy(new LinkedList&lt;&gt;()));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>index loop\niterator</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why were default methods added in Java 8?</summary><div><p>For backward-compatible interface evolution: new methods (like <code>Collection.stream()</code>) could be added with a body so existing implementing classes kept compiling. They also let interfaces offer convenience methods (<code>Comparator.reversed()</code>).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can an interface have a constructor or instance fields?</summary><div><p>No. Interfaces carry no per-object state; any field is implicitly <code>public static final</code>. That is exactly why multiple interface inheritance is safe.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can an interface extend another interface? A class?</summary><div><p>An interface can <code>extend</code> any number of interfaces (<code>interface C extends A, B</code>). It can never extend a class.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>If a class and an interface both provide the same method, which wins?</summary><div><p>The class. “Class wins” — a concrete (or inherited superclass) method always takes precedence over an interface default method.</p></div></details></section>"}